  "content": "# Travel Journal: The Ascent to Kharbu\n\n*October 14th*\n\nThe road ended where the map did, at a weathered wooden sign half-swallowed by moss, and from there the journey became a matter of boots and breath. I left the jeep at dawn, when the valley below still held a pale, milky light, and began the climb along a goat path that wound through birch forest. The air grew thinner and colder with every hundred meters of elevation, and the trees thinned from dense green to scattered, wind-bent trunks. My pack weighed perhaps fifteen kilograms\u2014sampling equipment, notebooks, thermos of tea that had already gone lukewarm. I stopped often, not from exhaustion but from the sheer need to watch, to record, to let the landscape register itself fully before I could reduce it to data.\n\nBy mid-morning the forest gave way entirely, and I emerged onto an alpine meadow streaked with late-blooming edelweiss and a species of gentian I did not immediately recognize. The sky above was a hard, uncompromising blue, the kind that only exists at altitude, and the clouds hung low enough to touch the distant ridgeline. I knelt in",
